---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import LinkCards from "../../components/docs/LinkCards.astro";
import { docHref } from "../../data/docs";
---

<DocsLayout slug="pull-requests">
  <section aria-labelledby="open">
    <h2 id="open">Open pull requests</h2>
    <p><strong>Pull requests</strong> lists your projects' pull requests on GitHub and lets you review them without leaving OpenOrc. It reads everything through your own GitHub CLI, <code>gh</code>. Your review stays a draft in OpenOrc, and nothing reaches GitHub until you submit it.</p>
    <p>Click <strong>Pull requests</strong> in the sidebar.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="browse">
    <h2 id="browse">Browse and filter</h2>
    <p>The page's title names the project it lists, or <strong>All projects</strong>: every project whose remote is on github.com, each under its own heading. After it comes the view, such as <strong>All pull requests</strong>.</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Control</th>
            <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>The state tabs</td>
            <td><strong>Open</strong>, <strong>Drafts</strong>, <strong>Merged</strong>, <strong>Closed</strong>, and <strong>All</strong>, each with how many pull requests it holds.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>The view, in the title</td>
            <td>A saved list. <strong>All pull requests</strong>, <strong>Needs my review</strong>, and <strong>Mine</strong> are built in. <strong>Save as a new view…</strong> keeps the filters you set under a name, and the same menu makes a view the one the page opens with, or deletes a view of yours.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Filter pull requests</strong></td>
            <td>Narrows the list by person, label, review, milestone, or project, as GitHub's own filters do. Words you type there can also search titles, numbers, authors, and branches. Each filter shows under the tabs, where you can remove it.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>The sort menu</td>
            <td>Orders the list, such as <strong>Newest</strong>, <strong>Recently updated</strong>, or <strong>Most commented</strong>.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Each row shows the title and its labels, then the number, the author, and the branch it merges from and into. At its end are a status, the lines added and removed, the number of comments, and when it last changed. The status is <strong>Open</strong>, <strong>Draft</strong>, <strong>Approved</strong>, <strong>Changes requested</strong>, <strong>Review required</strong>, <strong>Merged</strong>, or <strong>Closed</strong>. It names the reviewer while a model reviews, and counts your draft comments while a review of yours waits to be posted.</p>
    <p>A status is amber when the next move is yours, and those rows lead their project with a button for the move: <strong>Review</strong>, <strong>Finish review</strong>, or <strong>Open thread</strong>. Beside <strong>Review</strong>, a button named after the model or Orcling you last reviewed with starts that review at once. Point at a row for <strong>Copy link</strong> and <strong>Open in new tab</strong>. Each project lists up to 50 pull requests.</p>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: <code>apps/desktop/src/renderer/src/views/PullRequestList.tsx</code>, <code>components/PullRequestPalette.tsx</code>, <code>PullRequestViewMenu.tsx</code>, <code>lib/pull-requests.ts</code>, <code>packages/git/src/github.ts</code></p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="read">
    <h2 id="read">Read a pull request</h2>
    <p>Click a row to open it. The header shows its title, state, author, branches, line counts and number of files. Switch between <strong>Changes</strong>, the diff as GitHub shows it, and <strong>Description</strong>, the author's text. <strong>Open on GitHub</strong> opens it in your browser, and <strong>Refresh</strong> loads it again.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="draft">
    <h2 id="draft">Draft a review</h2>
    <p>You can write comments yourself whichever reviewer is chosen:</p>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>On <strong>Changes</strong>, click a line number. Drag or shift-click across line numbers to comment on several lines.</li>
      <li>Write the comment and choose <strong>Add comment</strong>.</li>
      <li>Repeat for each finding.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The bar under the diff counts your draft comments. Click the count to list them, and use the buttons beside each one to edit or remove it. A comment belongs to the commit it was written on. When the author pushes new commits, the bar warns that the comments were written on an earlier version and will post there. To comment on the new commits, post or discard the draft first.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="model">
    <h2 id="model">Let a model review</h2>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Set <strong>Reviewer</strong> to <strong>Model</strong>.</li>
      <li>Pick a model, or an <a href={docHref("orclings")}>Orcling</a> to review with its own model. For a Claude model, in a project that uses more than one Claude account, the picker also lists the accounts, and the review runs on the one you pick.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Start review</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The model reviews in a thread of its own, named <code>Review #&lt;number&gt;: &lt;title&gt;</code>. It works on a separate copy of the pull request on no branch, so your checkout isn't touched. The copy is readied like a new worktree, with your copied files, submodules, and the project's setup script, and the review starts in the mode your new threads start in, so the model can type-check, lint, test, and run the change before it writes its findings. A pull request from a fork is reviewed in Plan mode instead, where its code is read and not run; you can change the mode in the review thread. It records each finding as a draft comment on the changed line and finishes with a summary. Its comments join your draft, labeled with the model's name. A link beside <strong>Reviewer</strong> opens the review thread. It says the model is reviewing while it works, and reads <strong>Review conversation</strong> once it is done.</p>
    <p>After the author pushes again, choose <strong>Review again</strong>. The same thread continues on the new commits, checks whether its earlier findings were addressed and comments only on what is left or new. A draft with comments on the older commits has to be posted or discarded first. If you resolved the review thread, the next review starts a fresh one. So does a review on another Claude account than the thread's, which starts its thread on that account; while <strong>Keep accounts separate</strong> is on, post or discard a draft written on the thread's account first.</p>
    <p>The model can read the draft and reword or remove its comments, yours included, when you ask it to in the review thread. What it changes in its copy stays there. OpenOrc tells it not to fix the pull request, commit, push, or post to GitHub: its comments and summary are drafts, and you decide what to submit.</p>
    <p>For a project that is only on OpenOrc Cloud, with no folder on this computer, the review runs in a cloud thread, and the picker offers the models OpenOrc Cloud runs. OpenOrc Cloud keeps the draft. An Orcling can't review there.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="submit">
    <h2 id="submit">Submit the review</h2>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Choose <strong>Submit review</strong>.</li>
      <li>Write a <strong>Summary</strong>. A model's summary fills the field when you haven't written one; otherwise it appears below as <strong>Model's summary</strong>, with <strong>Add to summary</strong>.</li>
      <li>Under <strong>Post as</strong>, pick <strong>You</strong>, or <strong>Reviewer app</strong> if you set one up.</li>
      <li>Under <strong>Verdict</strong>, pick <strong>Comment</strong>, <strong>Approve</strong> or <strong>Request changes</strong>.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Post review</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>OpenOrc posts the summary, the verdict and every draft comment as one GitHub review, then clears what it posted from the draft. The bar then shows <strong>Review posted.</strong> and a <strong>View on GitHub</strong> link.</p>
    <p>A review needs a summary or at least one comment, unless you approve. When you post as yourself, you can't approve or request changes on your own pull request. <strong>Discard draft</strong> deletes the draft's comments and summary instead.</p>
    <Callout>You can't post or discard a draft while a model is still reviewing. Wait for it to finish, or stop it in its thread.</Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="requirements">
    <h2 id="requirements">What it needs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The GitHub CLI, installed and signed in. Run <code>gh auth login</code> in a terminal. Without <code>gh</code>, the view says <strong>GitHub CLI not found</strong>.</li>
      <li>A project whose remote is on GitHub, or a GitHub project on OpenOrc Cloud.</li>
      <li>To let a model review, an agent you are signed in to.</li>
      <li>To post as a bot, a reviewer app: in <strong>Settings</strong>, open <strong>Connections</strong>, find <strong>Reviewer app</strong> under GitHub and choose <strong>Set up</strong>. The app can approve only when <strong>Let it approve</strong> is on. See <a href={docHref("settings")}>Settings</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "review" }, { slug: "git" }, { slug: "agents" }]} columns={3} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
